    "body": "# What does this PR do?\r\n\r\nThe Windows section of the README only covers NVIDIA. LlamaFactory itself needs no changes to run on AMD GPUs under Windows: the ROCm build of PyTorch exposes the same `torch.cuda` API, and `bitsandbytes` has shipped Windows ROCm builds in its official releases since `0.50.2`. What was missing is how to install it.\r\n\r\nThis adds ROCm steps for AMD users to the Install PyTorch part of the Windows guide in `README.md` and `README_zh.md`. PyTorch comes from AMD's TheRock index using the documented `[device-gfxNNNN]` extras, which pull in the per-GPU kernel packages. Installing plain `torch` from that index gives a build that detects the GPU but fails at runtime with `hipErrorInvalidImage`. It also notes which GPUs need `TORCH_ROCM_AOTRITON_ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL=1` for the AOTriton flash and memory-efficient SDPA backends, since without it they fall back to the math backend, and that `bitsandbytes` covers AMD on Windows too.\r\n\r\nTested on Windows 11 with an RX 9060 XT (`gfx1200`), `torch 2.13.0+rocm10.0.0` and `bitsandbytes 0.50.2`: `llamafactory-cli env`, LoRA SFT and 4-bit QLoRA SFT on `Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct`, and inference through `ChatModel` with the trained adapter. A 30-step LoRA run took about 29 s.\r\n\r\n## Before submitting\r\n\r\n- [x] Did you read the [contributor guideline](https://github.com/hiyouga/LLaMA-Factory/blob/main/.github/CONTRIBUTING.md)?\r\n- [ ] Did you write any new necessary tests?",
